    {
      "hint_id": 2,
      "train_position": 28,
      "train_qid": "30302",
      "hint": "For root-locus questions, take moduli before hunting individual roots. Given (z-a)^n = c(z-b)^n, divide by (z-b)^n and take absolute values: |z-a| = k|z-b| with k = |c|^(1/n). That is an Apollonius circle, so every root lies on one circle whichever n-th root of c you pick; you never need the roots themselves. Avoid parametrizing the ratio as k e^{i t} and eliminating t, which is where sign and algebra slips occur. Instead set z = x+iy, square both sides, collect the x^2+y^2 terms, divide by their coefficient, and complete the square to read off center and radius. Note z = b is never a root, and k = 1 degenerates to a line. Confirm by substituting one real root into your circle equation.",
