      "train_qid": "25003",
      "hint": "Several hidden degree-n polynomials must jointly hit prescribed values at consecutive integers. Two traps: do NOT difference one polynomial and conclude impossibility, and do NOT assume each polynomial is used once.\n\nMethod: write the prescribed value as a function of the INPUT, not the position; for an arithmetic progression over consecutive inputs the target is affine, L(x)=px+q. Fix one polynomial P and set g=P-L. Every input assigned to P is a root of g. Compare degrees to show g is not identically zero, so g has at most n roots; that caps how many inputs any single polynomial can serve. Multiply/sum that cap over the family for the upper bound, then exhibit a construction giving each polynomial a disjoint block of inputs where it interpolates L. Report one exact integer.",
